Restricted Huawei DEVMAP_HASH candidate
=====================================

Base: 4ae5c61bd3260ee9b317489755fa144b306589ec
Reference: Pixel msm-4.14 bb17b7de4b570f2c0c32ddba0543cac03c58dd84

The preceding original-author commits import only hash control-plane functions
into an initially unwired devmap_hash.inc. Their original messages and trailers
are retained; *.patch.txt records the exact devmap.c source patches, including
structural hunks requiring separate integration. These intermediate commits are
provenance milestones, not standalone kernel candidates. The mechanical lock
insertion in the unwired fragment was caught by the host compile and corrected
in the Huawei integration. No new XDP frame/bulk queue implementation is imported.

Reference dispositions:
5ce6efdab: hash lookup, iteration, CRUD and allocation algorithms.
0cf25d6f: take writer lock before lookup/allocation/publication.
f51dc9cf: use u64 costs; integrated with fixed capacity below.
ab1efd14: unregister walks every bucket and removes every matching reference.
fb9bb964: separate hash destruction/storage; no array allocation for HASH.
d5cecfb2: this older RCU macro lacks the lockdep argument; callers already hold
          RCU (syscall and notifier). Writer paths additionally hold index_lock.
d9452bb6: bucket allocation/free via bpf_map_area_alloc/free.
ebeb7a6b: cast bucket allocation multiplication to u64.
fc81b674: capacity is exactly64; no power-of-two rounding/overflow is possible.
eaaabe4e: hash delete uses u32 keys. Existing array implementation is unchanged.

Huawei adaptation:
- Native map25, exactly key/value4 and capacity64; create flags must be0.
  Stored/info flags are BPF_F_RDONLY_PROG (0x80), not BPF_F_RDONLY. Userspace
  update/delete are allowed. No BTF association is enabled for this map type.
- Hash keys are unrestricted u32, including0 and0xffffffff. BPF_EXIST on a
  missing key returns ENOENT. Replacement does not consume another capacity slot.
- Writers serialize through index_lock, including device acquisition in the
  updating task's netns. Unregister runs under RCU and the same writer lock.
  Device pointer identity, not an ifindex in the reader's netns, governs removal.
- Every published entry owns one dev_get_by_index reference. Deletion,
  replacement, unregister, and full-insert failure queue a hash-only callback.
  That callback puts the reference and frees the entry, never touching bitmaps.
  Map destruction unlinks from the notifier list, waits for RCU readers, retains
  rcu_barrier(), and frees remaining live bucket entries directly.
- Existing array bitmap paths, entry callback and rcu_barrier are preserved.
  HASH allocates neither netdev_map nor flush_needed.
- check_map_func_compatibility rejects every helper for HASH in its first
  map-type switch. A map pointer may still be loaded, but cannot be passed to
  lookup/update/delete/redirect or other helpers. No new program-load ban.
  The verifier's pseudo-map LD_IMM records the map type; map-in-map propagation
  retains inner_map_meta's type. No BPF callback helper mechanism exists here.

Validation: scripts/tests/test_devmap_hash.py builds the entire devmap source
(including this fragment), the real map-info function, real helper compatibility
function and real registration dispatch under ASan/UBSan. Host boundaries model
netdevs/netns, allocation, locking, deferred RCU, usercopy and list primitives.
These are deterministic contract tests, not live kernel race/scheduler tests.
Main owns full kernel build, selected-loader reproduction and consumer ELF audit.
No active source pins or device state are changed by this candidate.
